This directory contains codes to extract and fixup the examples.

The LaTeX source must be annotated to mark the code fragments and to provide
details that are missing from the example (since many code examples do not
declare all variables or define complete functions).  The general approach is

%%HEADER
....
%%ENDHEADER
\begin{verbatim}
code
\end{verbatim}

This allows easy extraction of the code from the source files.  Commands
in the header allow you to specify how the code should be processed.

Commands include:

%%LANG: C
%%LANG: FORTRAN
%%LANG: FORTRAN90
%%LANG: FORTRAN08
%%LANG: C++
    Specify language
    If no LANG is specified, C is the default.

%%DECL:
    declarations to add
    For example,
    %%DECL: int a;

%%DECLAFTERMOD:
    declarations to add after a "use mpi" or "use mpi_f08" that appears in
    a program fragment. Only if %%NOADDMPIMOD is also set. For example,
    %%DECLAFTERMOD: TYPE(MPI_Comm) :: commold

%%FRAGMENT
    code is not an entire routine.  In this case, the code is placed into
    a function named "testroutine", which by default is typed as
    void testroutine(void).  The return type may be changed with %%RETURN: ;
    the arguments with %%ARGS:

%%SKIPELIPSIS
    ignore ... in code

%%SUBST:searchstring:replacement
    Edit the text.  For example,
    %%SUBST:/\* compute value \*/:1
    replaces
        if (/* compute value */) {
    with
        if (1) {
    (Yes, you need to escape perl regular-expresion characters like *)
    For example, to change ... to a,b,c use
    %%SUBST:\.\.\.:a,b,c

%%SKIP
    Skip the related verbatim environment
%%NOCHECK
    Ignore this verbatim

%%TAIL:
    Text to add at the end of the code
    Example:
    %%TAIL: return 0;}

%%NOUSEMPI
    In Fortran examples, do not replace mpif.h with "use mpi"

%%NOADDMPIMOD
    In Fortran examples, do not add a "use MPI" or "use MPI_F08" statuement
    This should be used only when the example fragment includes the appropriate
    use statement

%%NOIMPLICITNONE
    In Fortran examples that are code fragments, an IMPLICIT NONE is normally
    added after a "use mpi" or "use mpi_f08" (IMPLICIT NONE must occur after
    all use statements). It this command is present, no IMPLICIT NONE is
    added. This is needed for Fortran code fragments that have explicit "use"
    statements (and are most likely also marked with %%NOADDMPIMOD)

%%INCDIR:
    Specify a directory to add for the include search path
    For example,
    %%INCDIR ../include

%%TOP:
    Text to add at the top of the code
    For example,
    %%TOP: #include <unistd.h>

%%RETURN:
    Type of return from routine
    For example,
    %%HEADER
    %%LANG: C
    %%FRAGMENT
    %%RETURN: int
    %%ENDHEADER
    \begin{verbatim}
        if (!malloc(4)) return 1;
    \end{verbatim}

%%ARGS:
    Pass these arguments to the testroutine.  See FRAGMENT and RETURN.

%%MSG:string
    Output the string to stdout. This is useful for examples that are
    erroneous and are expected to generate a warning or error message, while
    allowing the rest of the example to be checked for correctness.
    Used in one example where scalars are passed to a Fortran routine that
    is expecting arrays.

%%AUTOCONTINUE
    For Fortran and Fortran90 only, this adds a Fortran (free format)
    continuation to lines that end in a comma, plus, or minus

%%ALLOWMISMATCH
    For Fortran (any version), attempt to add a compile-time option to
    allow argument mismatches. This is needed for some of the "choice"
    routines, when the Fortran modele doesn't handle this case. This may
    work only when such an option is available (and known) for the compiler
    being used.  E.g., for gfortran, -fallow-argument-mismatch

(Unimplemented)
%%ALLOWIMPLICIT
    For Fortran (any version), allow implicit function declarations.
    This should only be used when a non-MPI function is used, and
    including an interface declaration is considered distracting or
    unnecessary.

-------------
Once the source files have been annotated, the command mpichk should be used
to extract and compile the example code.  This command is created by configure,
as in

   ./configure

By default, it will attempt to find mpicc (or mpcc), mpif90 (or mpxlf90),
mpicxx (or mpixlC) and mpif77 (or mpxlf).  These can be overridden by defining
the environment variables MPICC, MPIF90, MPICXX, and MPIF77 (you can set
these on the configure command line).



Also useful, if the underlying compile command is gcc or gfortran, is

MPICHK_CFLAGS="-Wall -Wextra -Wstrict-prototypes -ansi"
MPICHK_CXXFLAGS="-Wall -Wextra -ansi"
MPICHK_F90FLAGS="-Wall -Wextra"

Depending on the version of gcc, you might want to use the MPICHK_CFLAGS value

-Werror-implicit-function-declaration

(Note that the misnamed -Wall does not enable all warnings, and the set of
warnings that it does enable can change between versions of gcc, so using
an explicit option to force these checks is safer.)

These will generate some warnings that may be ignored (e.g., warnings about
unused arguments in routines such as the attribute copy and delete functions,
where some of the arguments are unnneeded) but will also point out such things
as unused (and hence possibly confusing to the reader) variables.

To reject any MPI example that uses a deprected routine, define the
environment variable

MPICHK_NODEPRECATED=yes

Setting the environment variable MPICHK_VERBOSE will echo the command that
is being run for each test.

If you do not have a Fortran compiler, use --disable-fortran on the configure
line:

    ./configure --disable-fortran

The script will still extract the Fortan examples and create files with them,
but it will not attempt to compile them.

If you do use Fortran, there is a Fortran module that can be used to
you will need to either use a version of mpifort that supports all
features of MPI in the Fortran examples or build the MPI module in the
mpicompilechk/fortran directory.  Use

     cd fortran
     make

to build the MPI module.  At this time, an mpi_f08 module is not available.

To run the mpichk program, either use the target in the mpi-standard Makefile
(runs mpichk on all chapters) or use this command to run the check on a single
file:

   mpicompilechk/mpichk file.tex

e.g.,

   mpicompilechk/mpichk chap-pt2pt/pt2pt.tex

Sligbtly cleaner is to use the same options as in the Makefile:

   mpicompilechk/mpichk --outdir=chap-pt2pt chap-pt2pt/pt2pt.tex





